{
  "configuration_id": "config_030",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best configuration so far is config_018 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) at 0.619 acc. Across all 7 completed experiments, only pca_n2000 has been used as the RNA encoder, leaving the RNA representation axis entirely unexplored. RNA is the dominant signal (RNA-only multiprofile: 0.591 vs image-only: 0.208), and the multimodal gain over RNA-only is modest (~0.028), suggesting the RNA representation may be a bottleneck. Question: Does replacing linear PCA with a learned autoencoder (scvi_n200) improve the RNA representation enough to lift performance in the best-known multimodal multiprofile setup? config_030 changes only the RNA encoder (pca_n2000 → scvi_n200) while holding model setting, profile, image encoder, aggregation, and fusion fixed, isolating the encoder effect. This is the highest-information next step because the RNA encoder is the single largest untested design axis, and a positive result would redirect the search toward learned representations, while a null/negative result would confirm PCA is sufficient and shift focus to the image encoder or fusion strategy."
}

